          "distinct": false,
          "id": "9763305b895ce73b6a93635609b267283291ddac",
          "message": "feat(sparq-reason): adopt sparq_substrate::numeric as the N3 chainer arithmetic core (seam 2) [OPUS-4.8] (sq-pbz04.5.1) (#1867)\n\nThe N3 chainer's exact `math:` add/subtract/multiply/negate/abs core (and the\nscale-aligned comparison the max/min + value-equality paths use) now DELEGATES to\nthe SHARED `sparq_substrate::numeric::Dec` tower — the same exact fixed-point\n`mant * 10^-scale` decimal the SPARQL engine's FILTER/BIND path drives — so the\nreasoner and the engine can never diverge on exact-decimal arithmetic. This closes\nthe epic's \"seam-2\" gap: RIF builtins lower to N3 builtins, so one adoption serves\nboth dialects with a single evaluation path.\n\nThe private `NumVal` enum stays a thin EYE-compat ADAPTER over that core. Every\nEYE-specific edge is preserved BYTE-FOR-BYTE and stays adapter-resident: lexical-shape\nstring coercion (`numval`), the `numval_term` result rendering (whole-`f64` →\n`xsd:integer`, `Dec` normalised via `dec_norm`), `math:remainder`'s divisor-sign\ninteger semantics, `math:integerQuotient`'s aligned-mantissa floor, `math:quotient`'s\nscale-34 exactness rule (non-terminating → `f64`, exact integer/integer → `xsd:integer`,\nNOT substrate `Dec::checked_div`'s always-decimal scale-18 rounding), integer\n`math:exponentiation`, and the `Int`-collapse rendering of floor/ceiling. Those edges\nare a REASONED non-adoption of the substrate's differing method contracts — they keep\ntheir EYE algorithm rather than smuggle a behaviour change through a shared method.\n\nThe i128↔i64 wrinkle (bead): the chainer `Int` tier is `i128` while substrate\n`Num::Int` is `i64`, so this adapter never carries a substrate `Num::Int` — it converts\neach exact tier to a substrate `Dec` for the delegated arithmetic (`Int(i)` →\n`Dec { mant: i, scale: 0 }`, exact for the full `i128` range incl. `> i64::MAX`; a\nmantissa overflow falls back to `f64` exactly as the pre-adoption `checked_*` path did).\n\nCargo.toml: `sparq-substrate` becomes a NON-optional dep with `default-features = false`\n`features = [\"numeric\"]` (the deviation from \"opt-in throughout\" is argued in the record\n§2 — sparq-reason is itself the opt-in surface and is never in the wasm graph; a\nfeature-gated dual evaluator is worse). `rows`/`join`/`compare` slices stay behind the\nconsumer features (`substrate-join`/`substrate-compare`/`compiled-rules`/`d-entail`).\n\nINVARIANT (behaviour-neutral): the N3/EYE differential floors, the N3 expressivity\nfloors, and `RIF_CORE_FLOOR` are all byte-identical — the closure is unchanged. A NEW\ndirect old-`NumVal`-vs-substrate differential (`substrate_seam_differential`) pins the\narithmetic matrix the bead names: `> i64::MAX` integers, exact-decimal add/sub/mul\n(`0.1 + 0.2` = `0.3`), INF/NaN, and the `('2.7' '2') math:difference = 0.7` exactness\ncase. No `Box<dyn>`/`&dyn` (check-no-dyn-dispatch.py green); lean core untouched.\n\nGates: clippy --workspace -D warnings, sparq-reason tests (default / all-features /\nno-default-features), rustdoc --all-features -D warnings, check-no-dyn-dispatch.py,\nreadme-template (0 dev), RIF_CORE_FLOOR unchanged, coverage floor held.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5 <jw896977@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
